course: dictionary
lesson: 6690
title: One word, many meanings: occasion
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Occasion is a good example. It can be a time when something happens, a special event, or even a reason. The words around it are its context, so read them carefully.
explain: «On this occasion we're not arguing with you; we're just telling you.» Here occasion means a particular time, one moment in a longer story. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? You could say "on this time", and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Compare that with «a birthday is a special occasion». Now occasion means a special event or celebration, something people plan and enjoy. When you meet a word with several meanings, try each one in the sentence and keep the one that fits.
fact: Occasion is a noun: an occasion, or several occasions.
fact: An occasion can be a particular time, as in «on this occasion».
fact: A special occasion is an event or celebration, such as a birthday, a wedding or a holiday.
fact: «On occasion» means sometimes, and «rise to the occasion» means do well when it matters.
fact: «Occasion» has two c's and one s.
exercise: «It was a special occasion, so we wore our best clothes.» What is an occasion here? | * A special event or celebration | A map of the town | A reason to worry | A kind of clothes
exercise: Mum says, «There is no occasion to worry.» What does occasion mean here? | A time on the clock | Something that is cooked | * A reason or cause | A party
exercise: «On occasion, Dad makes pancakes.» What does on occasion mean? | Every single day | * Sometimes | Never at all | At the very end
test: The storm occasioned a long delay to the trains. What does occasioned mean? | Ended | Celebrated | * Caused | Forgot
test: «Occasion» has two c's and one s. How many letters are in the word occasion? | 7 | 10 | * 8 | 9
exam: When Sam was asked to sing at the concert, she rose to the occasion and did her best. What does rose to the occasion mean? | * Did well when it mattered | Ran away from the stage | Forgot all the words | Sang very quietly
